<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Terai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Terai is a belt of lowland plains, marshes and forest along the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, mostly in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and the adjoining parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Once malarial and thinly settled, it was home to the Shakya and Koliya republics and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, the Buddha's birthplace. Gorkha expansion there led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814–1816, and it is now Nepal's main farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/terai.jpg" alt="Terai" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barunkhanal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terai_nepal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A small British expedition under Captain George Kinloch, sent in 1767 to help the Newar king Jaya Prakash Malla, had already failed in the <b>Terai</b> lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>One branch took the hill fort of Makwanpur and the adjacent lowlands of the <b>Terai</b>, controlling the main route from Bihar to the Kathmandu Valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-makwanpur">Kingdom of Makwanpur</a></li><li><blockquote>The Koliyas lived east of the Shakya republic, across the Rohini river in the <b>Terai</b> of modern Nepal and India.</blockquote><a href="/state/koliya-republic">Koliya republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Shakyas were a clan claiming descent from the Ikshvaku kings, settled in the <b>Terai</b> on the border of modern Nepal and India.</blockquote><a href="/state/shakya-republic">Shakya republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom that grew there lay in what is now northern Bihar and the <b>Terai</b> of Nepal, and it was one of the easternmost Vedic states.</blockquote><a href="/state/videha">Videha</a></li></ul></section>
